# Shell scripts must reach macOS with LF endings. A CRLF script makes zsh report
# "command not found" for commands that plainly exist, and the tester cannot see that the
# error is about the file rather than the command.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Luau, and everything else text, is stored with LF.
#
# The repository was already mostly LF — eighteen of twenty-two module sources — and the four
# that were not made every edit to them look like a whole-file rewrite, because the tooling
# here writes LF. That is not a cosmetic problem: a 3700-line file that appears entirely
# changed hides the four lines that actually changed, in exactly the review where seeing them
# matters.
#
# Normalised on commit rather than by a hook. A hook lives in .git/hooks, which is not cloned
# and not pushed, so a second machine or the macOS runner would quietly not have it; this file
# is committed and applies wherever the repository goes. It is also the mechanism already in
# use directly above, for the same reason.
*.luau text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
